From c90ea056bd5bcacd0417389287ccdf23788af55c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cpkt9762 <cpkt9762@163.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 16:57:45 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(cloud): keep pulling when push fails

Forward-port of fork patch 0010. Upstream still short-circuits: cycle()
returns as soon as push returns an error, so the pull never runs.

The failure mode is that one project takes the whole machine down. A project
that is not enrolled makes push return nonEnrolledPendingError; recordBlocked
then sets BackoffUntil to nil, so every poll retries immediately and fails for
the same reason forever. The node silently stops receiving remote memories
with no backoff to recover from. Observed on a WSL box: eight pending
solana-arb-bot mutations, last_pulled_seq frozen at 27562 until
`engram cloud enroll` cleared the block.

Push and pull are independent rails. A node that cannot push still needs to
receive. Pull now runs unconditionally and the push error is reported after.

recordFailureWithReason takes the failed phase explicitly instead of inferring
it from m.status.Phase. With pull running first, the phase is PhasePulling by
the time a push error is recorded, so the old inference would have relabelled
every push failure as a pull failure -- the sort of change that looks harmless
and quietly destroys the ability to tell the two apart in the field.

One upstream test asserted the behaviour this patch removes.
TestManagerCyclePartialPushFailureSkipsPullAndHealthyState bundled three
claims: the failure is recorded with backoff, the pull is skipped, and the
cycle is not marked healthy. The first and third still hold and are untouched;
only the second is inverted, and the test is renamed so its name stops
describing behaviour the fork no longer has. This is a deliberate divergence
from upstream, not a merge artifact.

Mutation-checked: restoring the short-circuit fails five tests including the
renamed one and TestManagerPullsEvenWhenPushFails.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
